<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Atsiz</h1><p class="st-meta">1071 CE – 1079 CE · capital Damascus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Turkish state in southern Syria and Palestine, carved out by the Turkmen commander Atsiz at the expense of the Fatimids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atsiz-ibn-uvaq">Atsiz ibn Uvaq</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander who entered Fatimid service as Turkish bands spread into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk</a> victories. Around 1071 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> for himself, and in 1076 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, proclaiming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and the Seljuk sultan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His rule rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> warriors and was harsh: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> suffered famine and extortion, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> rebelled in 1077 he retook it and massacred many inhabitants. In 1077 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> but was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. His state was a military domain with little administration of its own.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Threatened by a Fatimid counter-offensive, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atsiz-ibn-uvaq">Atsiz</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a> for help. The sultan sent his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush</a>, who had Atsiz killed in 1079 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-syria">Seljuk Sultanate of Syria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1071 CE – 1079 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.01%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1071 CE – 1079 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Damascus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Atsiz ibn Uvaq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria, Israel, Palestine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Atsiz ibn Uvaq's emirate</dd></dl></aside></div>
